1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,240
robots were stealing jobs back in 1830.

2
00:00:03,240 --> 00:00:06,679
Hello everyone and welcome to this new deep dive.

3
00:00:06,679 --> 00:00:12,019
You know, today we hear constantly about algorithms and artificial intelligence

4
00:00:12,019 --> 00:00:14,880
that will sooner or later take away our jobs, right?

5
00:00:14,880 --> 00:00:16,760
It is a very widespread anxiety,

6
00:00:16,760 --> 00:00:20,460
but what if I told you that this exact same dynamic,

7
00:00:20,460 --> 00:00:23,039
this terrible fear, had already been unleashed,

8
00:00:23,039 --> 00:00:26,560
in a literally explosive way, almost two hundred years ago?

9
00:00:26,560 --> 00:00:31,360
Exactly, let's take a leap back in time, to the 1830s,

10
00:00:31,360 --> 00:00:34,359
to solve a real economic mystery.

11
00:00:34,359 --> 00:00:35,719
Get comfortable.

12
00:00:35,719 --> 00:00:38,320
The question that torments us today is always the same:

13
00:00:38,320 --> 00:00:42,240
will artificial intelligence really destroy our jobs?

14
00:00:42,240 --> 00:00:46,359
Well, if we look into the eyes of English farm workers from two centuries ago,

15
00:00:46,359 --> 00:00:48,640
we find exactly the same terror.

16
00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:52,560
Imagine the scene: suddenly a new technology arrives,

17
00:00:52,600 --> 00:00:54,080
almost alien to them,

18
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:56,560
which promises to do their exact same job,

19
00:00:56,560 --> 00:00:59,440
but at crazy speeds and without ever getting tired.

20
00:00:59,440 --> 00:01:03,400
Except, back then they weren't talking about software written in Silicon Valley,

21
00:01:03,400 --> 00:01:06,159
they were talking about newly invented agricultural machines,

22
00:01:06,159 --> 00:01:10,040
and I assure you their reaction was not peaceful at all.

23
00:01:10,040 --> 00:01:12,159
In fact, quite the opposite.

24
00:01:12,159 --> 00:01:16,760
And the scale of this reaction can be summarized by an impressive number:

25
00:01:16,760 --> 00:01:17,760
3,000.

26
00:01:17,760 --> 00:01:20,800
Between 1830 and 1832,

27
00:01:20,840 --> 00:01:24,159
England was literally overwhelmed by what we know today

28
00:01:24,159 --> 00:01:26,159
as the Captain Swing riots.

29
00:01:26,159 --> 00:01:29,039
We are talking about over 3,000 documented incidents.

30
00:01:29,039 --> 00:01:33,200
Serious stuff: arson, looting, attacks on local authorities,

31
00:01:33,200 --> 00:01:37,640
and above all, the systematic and furious destruction of these new machines.

32
00:01:37,640 --> 00:01:41,599
A rebellion that spread like wildfire across 45 counties.

33
00:01:41,599 --> 00:01:44,079
The largest wave of protests in the country's history,

34
00:01:44,079 --> 00:01:47,359
so much so that in the end they had to send in the army to calm them down.

35
00:01:47,400 --> 00:01:51,280
But who was to blame for all this chaos?

36
00:01:51,280 --> 00:01:56,200
Let me introduce our suspect number one, the threshing machine.

37
00:01:56,200 --> 00:02:00,319
Basically, a mechanical gadget invented to do a simple,

38
00:02:00,319 --> 00:02:02,079
but extremely tiring thing:

39
00:02:02,079 --> 00:02:04,840
separating wheat grains from the chaff.

40
00:02:04,840 --> 00:02:08,039
Before, this work was strictly done by hand,

41
00:02:08,039 --> 00:02:11,719
toiling for hours with a tool called a flail.

42
00:02:11,719 --> 00:02:14,199
In short, a backbreaking struggle.

43
00:02:14,239 --> 00:02:16,399
The threshing machine was, if you think about it,

44
00:02:16,399 --> 00:02:19,439
the Terminator of 19th-century agriculture:

45
00:02:19,439 --> 00:02:22,399
whether it was powered by horses or water power,

46
00:02:22,399 --> 00:02:27,239
it managed to multiply a single worker's productivity by up to 10 times.

47
00:02:27,239 --> 00:02:29,679
A true masterpiece of engineering, sure,

48
00:02:29,679 --> 00:02:32,239
but at a very high human cost.

49
00:02:32,239 --> 00:02:35,599
And that brings us to the economic motive,

50
00:02:35,599 --> 00:02:38,359
the one that really triggers the spark.

51
00:02:38,359 --> 00:02:42,319
You see, manual threshing wasn't just any odd job.

52
00:02:42,359 --> 00:02:44,719
It was a literal lifeline

53
00:02:44,719 --> 00:02:46,599
for farm workers during the winter months,

54
00:02:46,599 --> 00:02:51,400
accounting for up to 50% of their seasonal income.

55
00:02:51,400 --> 00:02:53,439
50%!

56
00:02:53,439 --> 00:02:55,239
With the arrival of threshing machines,

57
00:02:55,239 --> 00:02:56,879
BAM, that vital income

58
00:02:56,879 --> 00:02:58,519
vanishes into thin air.

59
00:02:58,519 --> 00:03:00,879
In areas where these machines took hold,

60
00:03:00,879 --> 00:03:04,799
winter unemployment spiked to 7.6%.

61
00:03:04,799 --> 00:03:08,479
Compared to 5.5% in unaffected areas.

62
00:03:08,479 --> 00:03:11,199
So it wasn't just a matter of changing the way they worked.

63
00:03:11,240 --> 00:03:14,400
These machines were literally taking food out of the mouths

64
00:03:14,400 --> 00:03:17,480
of entire families during the harsh winter.

65
00:03:17,480 --> 00:03:19,560
And if you want the smoking gun,

66
00:03:19,560 --> 00:03:22,240
take a look at this correlation.

67
00:03:22,240 --> 00:03:25,480
The geographical overlap is almost striking.

68
00:03:25,480 --> 00:03:28,720
Sifting through period newspapers and agricultural records,

69
00:03:28,720 --> 00:03:31,920
researchers have reconstructed a crystal-clear picture.

70
00:03:31,920 --> 00:03:35,080
In parishes where threshing machines had never arrived,

71
00:03:35,080 --> 00:03:37,360
the probability of a riot breaking out

72
00:03:37,360 --> 00:03:40,320
hovered around 13.6%.

73
00:03:40,359 --> 00:03:43,159
But in areas where this technology was rampant?

74
00:03:43,159 --> 00:03:47,400
That rate doubled, easily exceeding 26%.

75
00:03:47,400 --> 00:03:50,359
In short, riots exploded exactly

76
00:03:50,359 --> 00:03:55,079
and almost surgically in the places where these new machines were installed.

77
00:03:55,079 --> 00:03:57,079
But wait a moment.

78
00:03:57,079 --> 00:03:59,799
Anyone who knows a bit about numbers knows that at this point

79
00:03:59,799 --> 00:04:03,439
we are facing a classic, huge puzzle.

80
00:04:03,439 --> 00:04:07,879
Are we looking at a simple correlation, or is there true causality?

81
00:04:07,919 --> 00:04:11,039
Was it really the machines causing the riots?

82
00:04:11,039 --> 00:04:14,120
Or think about it: maybe the landowners,

83
00:04:14,120 --> 00:04:15,960
who were certainly not naive,

84
00:04:15,960 --> 00:04:18,560
purposely avoided buying expensive machinery

85
00:04:18,560 --> 00:04:20,920
in areas they already knew were turbulent

86
00:04:20,920 --> 00:04:22,480
and at risk of rebellion.

87
00:04:22,480 --> 00:04:25,560
If so, we would be reading the data backwards.

88
00:04:25,560 --> 00:04:27,159
It's a bit like playing detective

89
00:04:27,159 --> 00:04:29,800
and having to reconstruct the exact sequence of events

90
00:04:29,800 --> 00:04:32,279
so as not to make a huge blunder.

91
00:04:32,279 --> 00:04:34,040
And here, the authors of the study

92
00:04:34,040 --> 00:04:37,000
pull an econometric trick out of their hat

93
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:38,879
that is simply brilliant:

94
00:04:38,879 --> 00:04:41,639
they used the earth, the actual soil,

95
00:04:41,639 --> 00:04:43,120
to unmask the truth,

96
00:04:43,120 --> 00:04:46,199
using it as an instrumental variable.

97
00:04:46,199 --> 00:04:49,839
I know "instrumental variable" sounds like a dusty jargon word

98
00:04:49,839 --> 00:04:51,279
from a statistics textbook,

99
00:04:51,279 --> 00:04:53,519
but trust me, it is the key to everything.

100
00:04:53,519 --> 00:04:55,879
Using modern geological maps

101
00:04:55,879 --> 00:04:57,160
that tell us exactly

102
00:04:57,160 --> 00:04:59,600
how the original soil was composed,

103
00:04:59,600 --> 00:05:01,360
a detail that in a couple of centuries

104
00:05:01,360 --> 00:05:03,560
obviously hasn't changed one bit,

105
00:05:03,560 --> 00:05:05,519
the researchers managed to isolate

106
00:05:05,519 --> 00:05:07,319
the arrival of technology,

107
00:05:07,319 --> 00:05:09,919
separating it entirely from the pre-existing risk

108
00:05:09,919 --> 00:05:11,359
of any riots.

109
00:05:11,359 --> 00:05:12,639
Brilliant, right?

110
00:05:12,639 --> 00:05:15,240
Let's follow the logic together step by step

111
00:05:15,240 --> 00:05:17,240
because it is truly fascinating.

112
00:05:17,240 --> 00:05:18,919
First: heavy soils,

113
00:05:18,919 --> 00:05:20,319
those full of clay,

114
00:05:20,319 --> 00:05:22,399
retain a lot of water.

115
00:05:22,399 --> 00:05:25,719
Second: because of this characteristic,

116
00:05:25,719 --> 00:05:27,919
they are terrible for growing wheat,

117
00:05:27,919 --> 00:05:30,680
so farmers preferred to use them for grazing

118
00:05:30,680 --> 00:05:32,639
or to plant other grains.

119
00:05:32,639 --> 00:05:34,799
Third, and here is the turning point:

120
00:05:34,800 --> 00:05:37,759
back then threshing machines cost an arm and a leg,

121
00:05:37,759 --> 00:05:41,160
and they were effective solely and exclusively for wheat.

122
00:05:41,160 --> 00:05:43,160
So, a very simple equation:

123
00:05:43,160 --> 00:05:45,800
no wheat equals no threshing machines,

124
00:05:45,800 --> 00:05:48,560
and fourth, no machines, no riots.

125
00:05:48,560 --> 00:05:51,160
In practice, it was literally geology

126
00:05:51,160 --> 00:05:53,360
deciding where technology ended up,

127
00:05:53,360 --> 00:05:55,240
and social anger could not influence

128
00:05:55,240 --> 00:05:58,080
this natural process in the slightest.

129
00:05:58,080 --> 00:05:59,800
And the sifted data,

130
00:05:59,800 --> 00:06:02,040
using this method, speak very clearly.

131
00:06:02,040 --> 00:06:04,639
We have unequivocal proof of causality:

132
00:06:04,639 --> 00:06:06,680
the more clayey and heavy the soil is,

133
00:06:06,680 --> 00:06:08,560
the more the use of threshing machines drops,

134
00:06:08,560 --> 00:06:09,560
and, guess what,

135
00:06:09,560 --> 00:06:11,399
they drop exactly in parallel,

136
00:06:11,399 --> 00:06:13,519
with the Captain Swing riots.

137
00:06:13,519 --> 00:06:15,519
Statistical analyses confirm

138
00:06:15,519 --> 00:06:17,680
that each additional machine installed,

139
00:06:17,680 --> 00:06:19,680
solely and exclusively because the land

140
00:06:19,680 --> 00:06:21,519
was perfect for wheat,

141
00:06:21,519 --> 00:06:23,240
caused a measurable spike

142
00:06:23,240 --> 00:06:24,599
in social unrest.

143
00:06:24,599 --> 00:06:25,719
There's no escape:

144
00:06:25,719 --> 00:06:27,519
technology that destroyed jobs

145
00:06:27,519 --> 00:06:30,240
mathematically led to social conflict.

146
00:06:30,240 --> 00:06:32,439
But history certainly doesn't stop here,

147
00:06:32,439 --> 00:06:33,919
there is a fundamental detail

148
00:06:33,960 --> 00:06:34,800
that is missing:

149
00:06:34,800 --> 00:06:36,879
this gigantic technological shock

150
00:06:36,879 --> 00:06:39,120
affected all of English agriculture,

151
00:06:39,120 --> 00:06:41,680
yet the countryside didn't burn everywhere.

152
00:06:41,680 --> 00:06:43,520
There were areas where the arrival

153
00:06:43,520 --> 00:06:46,040
of these mechanical beasts was welcomed,

154
00:06:46,040 --> 00:06:47,600
maybe not by popping champagne,

155
00:06:47,600 --> 00:07:00,400
but at least in a peaceful and resigned way.

156
00:07:00,400 --> 00:07:01,520
So, why?

157
00:07:01,520 --> 00:07:03,080
What was it that turned a county

158
00:07:03,080 --> 00:07:05,000
into a powder keg ready to explode,

159
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:06,680
while just a few miles away

160
00:07:06,680 --> 00:07:08,040
not a fly buzzed?

161
00:07:08,040 --> 00:07:09,800
To understand this, we have to widen our view

162
00:07:09,800 --> 00:07:01,800
and understand what was happening around

163
00:07:01,800 --> 00:07:03,319
these poor workers

164
00:07:03,319 --> 00:07:05,079
replaced by machines.

165
00:07:05,079 --> 00:07:07,800
The researchers show us that essentially

166
00:07:07,800 --> 00:07:09,680
there were two paths ahead of them,

167
00:07:09,680 --> 00:07:11,879
concepts that in economics we often call

168
00:07:11,879 --> 00:07:14,519
exit and, well, despair.

169
00:07:14,519 --> 00:07:15,879
The 'exit', the first path,

170
00:07:15,879 --> 00:07:17,759
was simply packing their bags.

171
00:07:17,759 --> 00:07:19,680
In areas near the rapidly expanding

172
00:07:19,680 --> 00:07:20,719
new industrial cities,

173
00:07:20,719 --> 00:07:22,039
those who lost their jobs in the fields

174
00:07:22,039 --> 00:07:24,759
could emigrate and seek their fortune in the factories.

175
00:07:24,759 --> 00:07:26,039
This safety valve

176
00:07:26,039 --> 00:07:28,399
drastically reduced tensions.

177
00:07:28,399 --> 00:07:29,639
The other side of the coin,

178
00:07:29,639 --> 00:07:31,800
unfortunately, was pure despair,

179
00:07:31,800 --> 00:07:34,160
worsened by the so-called enclosures.

180
00:07:34,160 --> 00:07:35,560
When historic common lands

181
00:07:35,560 --> 00:07:37,160
were fenced off by owners

182
00:07:37,160 --> 00:07:38,480
and taken away from public use,

183
00:07:38,480 --> 00:07:40,040
laborers also lost the right

184
00:07:40,040 --> 00:07:41,600
to graze their only cow

185
00:07:41,600 --> 00:07:43,360
or to gather firewood.

186
00:07:43,360 --> 00:07:45,480
Their last safety net was taken away,

187
00:07:45,480 --> 00:07:46,680
and, without a plan B,

188
00:07:46,680 --> 00:07:49,280
violence remained the only option on the table.

189
00:07:49,280 --> 00:07:51,080
These two contextual factors

190
00:07:51,080 --> 00:07:53,360
can be clearly read in the graphs.

191
00:07:53,360 --> 00:07:56,319
On one hand, there is a clear mitigating effect:

192
00:07:56,319 --> 00:07:58,280
the further away they were from the factories,

193
00:07:58,280 --> 00:08:00,720
and thus completely without exits,

194
00:08:00,720 --> 00:08:03,600
the more the probability of machines causing riots

195
00:08:03,600 --> 00:08:05,360
skyrocketed.

196
00:08:05,360 --> 00:08:08,320
On the other hand, there is a brutal aggravating effect:

197
00:08:08,320 --> 00:08:12,120
where enclosures had taken common lands away from workers,

198
00:08:12,120 --> 00:08:14,120
the link between the arrival of threshing machines

199
00:08:14,120 --> 00:08:16,880
and the outbreaks of rebellion was very strong.

200
00:08:16,880 --> 00:08:20,600
In short, combining an absolute lack of alternative work

201
00:08:20,600 --> 00:08:23,200
with the dismantling of basic rural welfare,

202
00:08:23,200 --> 00:08:26,800
resulted in the perfect explosive mix for unrest.

203
00:08:26,800 --> 00:08:28,120
Let's sum things up,

204
00:08:28,120 --> 00:08:31,800
because this historical inquiry gives us some incredible lessons.

205
00:08:31,800 --> 00:08:35,799
At a macroeconomic level, technology is undoubtedly a positive shock,

206
00:08:35,799 --> 00:08:37,720
it enormously increases productivity

207
00:08:37,720 --> 00:08:39,600
and the overall economic pie,

208
00:08:39,600 --> 00:08:41,320
and this is undeniable.

209
00:08:41,320 --> 00:08:44,000
But going down to the local level, in the short term,

210
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:45,799
it creates what in jargon we call

211
00:08:45,799 --> 00:08:48,159
a brutal distributional shock.

212
00:08:48,159 --> 00:08:51,480
Wealth shifts quickly from human labor to capital,

213
00:08:51,480 --> 00:08:54,039
wiping out the income of thousands of people,

214
00:08:54,039 --> 00:08:56,759
and when jobs evaporate rapidly

215
00:08:56,799 --> 00:09:00,200
and there are no valid safety nets and real alternatives,

216
00:09:00,200 --> 00:09:02,960
history teaches us that the almost guaranteed result

217
00:09:02,960 --> 00:09:05,559
is massive political instability.

218
00:09:05,559 --> 00:09:10,279
And after all, that is exactly what economics heavyweights have always told us,

219
00:09:10,279 --> 00:09:13,960
think of giants like Marx, Keynes, or Leontief,

220
00:09:13,960 --> 00:09:16,639
people with practically opposite worldviews,

221
00:09:16,639 --> 00:09:20,919
who nevertheless all agreed on this specific, crucial point.

222
00:09:20,919 --> 00:09:23,559
They had warned us that technological unemployment

223
00:09:23,559 --> 00:09:26,360
is not just a boring statistic in textbooks,

224
00:09:26,360 --> 00:09:29,639
but a profound risk to the very stability of a society.

225
00:09:29,639 --> 00:09:31,720
And the data from the Captain Swing riots

226
00:09:31,720 --> 00:09:35,800
give us almost ruthless empirical confirmation of these ancient fears.

227
00:09:35,800 --> 00:09:38,519
The shock generated by a disruptive technology

228
00:09:38,519 --> 00:09:42,519
is without a doubt one of the most universal economic challenges ever.

229
00:09:42,519 --> 00:09:45,720
Which, inevitably, brings us back to the present day,

230
00:09:45,720 --> 00:09:48,200
brings us back to artificial intelligence.

231
00:09:48,200 --> 00:09:52,360
Today AI promises to work miracles in many sectors,

232
00:09:52,360 --> 00:09:57,480
moving in a way incredibly similar to that of the threshing machine among the ears of wheat.

233
00:09:57,480 --> 00:10:03,080
But the real lesson we take home from that turbulent 1830 is very clear:

234
00:10:03,080 --> 00:10:06,039
it is not technology itself that creates chaos,

235
00:10:06,039 --> 00:10:08,840
it is the despair of those who have no alternatives.

236
00:10:08,840 --> 00:10:11,080
And so, while we see these innovations

237
00:10:11,080 --> 00:10:13,240
reshaping our world forever,

238
00:10:13,240 --> 00:10:15,720
the capital-Q question remains one.

239
00:10:15,720 --> 00:10:19,000
Do our societies of today have the safety nets

240
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:21,639
and the ability to reinvent themselves necessary

241
00:10:21,639 --> 00:10:24,279
to avoid the Captain Swings of the twenty-first century?

242
00:10:24,279 --> 00:10:26,360
I leave you with this food for thought.

243
00:10:26,360 --> 00:10:27,559
Until next time!